<section xml:id="sec-subspaces">
  <title>Subspaces</title>

  <objectives>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Learn the definition of a subspace.</p></li>
      <li><p>Learn to determine whether or not a subset is a subspace.</p></li>
      <li><p>Learn the most important examples of subspaces.</p></li>
      <li><p>Learn to write a given subspace as a column space or null space.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Recipe:</em> compute a spanning set for a null space.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Picture:</em> whether a subset of <m>\R^2</m> or <m>\R^3</m> is a subspace or not.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Vocabulary words:</em> <term>subspace</term>, <term>column space</term>, <term>null space</term>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section we discuss <em>subspaces</em> of <m>\R^n</m>.  A subspace turns out to be exactly the same thing as a span, except we don<rsq/>t have a particular set of spanning vectors in mind.  This change in perspective is quite useful, as it is easy to produce subspaces that are not obviously spans.  For example, the solution set of the equation <m>x + 3y + z = 0</m> is a span because the equation is homogeneous, but we would have to compute the parametric vector form in order to write it as a span.
      </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[myxyz, z={(0cm,0.8cm)}, scale=.75]
  \def\v{(2,-1,1)}
  \def\w{(1,0,-1)}

  \node[coordinate] (X) at \v {};
  \node[coordinate] (Y) at \w {};

  \begin{scope}[x=(X), y=(Y), transformxy]
