  \point at (0,0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
          ]]></latex-image>
</image>
<p>
      (A subspace also turns out to be the same thing as the solution set of a homogeneous system of equations.)
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-subspaces-definition-and-examples">
    <title>Subspaces: Definition and Examples</title>

    <definition>
      <idx><h>Subset</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>A <term>subset</term> of <m>\R^n</m> is any collection of points of <m>\R^n</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For instance, the unit circle
      <md>
<mrow>C = \bigl\{ (x,y)\text{ in }\R^2 \bigm| x^2 + y^2 = 1 \bigr\}</mrow>
</md>
      is a subset of <m>\R^2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Above we expressed <m>C</m> in <xref ref="set-builder-notation" text="title">set builder notation</xref>: in English, it reads <q><m>C</m> is the set of all ordered pairs <m>(x,y)</m> in <m>\R^2</m> such that <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>.</q>
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="subspaces-defn-of">
      <idx><h>Subspace</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>subspace</term> of <m>\R^n</m> is a subset <m>V</m> of <m>\R^n</m> satisfying:
          <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Non-emptiness:</alert> The zero vector is in <m>V</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Closure under addition:</alert> If <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are in <m>V</m>, then <m>u+v</m> is also in <m>V</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Closure under scalar multiplication:</alert> If <m>v</m> is in <m>V</m> and <m>c</m> is in <m>\R</m>, then <m>cv</m> is also in <m>V</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>As a consequence of these properties, we see:
    <ul>
      <li><p>If <m>v</m> is a vector in <m>V</m>, then all scalar multiples of <m>v</m> are in <m>V</m> by the third property.  In other words the line through any nonzero vector in <m>V</m> is also contained in <m>V</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>If <m>u,v</m> are vectors in <m>V</m> and <m>c,d</m> are scalars, then <m>cu,dv</m> are also in <m>V</m> by the third property, so <m>cu+dv</m> is in <m>V</m> by the second property.  Therefore, all of <m>\Span\{u,v\}</m> is contained in <m>V</m></p></li>
      <li><p>Similarly, if <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m> are all in <m>V</m>, then <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n\}</m> is contained in <m>V</m>.  In other words, <em>a subspace contains the span of any vectors in it.</em></p></li>
    </ul>
    If you choose enough vectors, then eventually their span will fill up <m>V</m>, so we already see that a <em>subspace is a span</em>.  See <xref ref="subspaces-spans-are-subspaces"/> below for a precise statement.
    </p>

    <remark>
      <p>Suppose that <m>V</m> is a non-empty subset of <m>\R^n</m> that satisfies properties 2 and 3.  Let <m>v</m> be any vector in <m>V</m>.  Then <m>0v = 0</m> is in <m>V</m> by the third property, so <m>V</m> automatically satisfies property 1.  It follows that the only subset of <m>\R^n</m> that satisfies properties 2 and 3 but <em>not</em> property 1 is the empty subset <m>\{\}</m>.  This is why we call the first property <q>non-emptiness</q>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <observation>
      <idx><h>Real <m>n</m>-space</h><h>as a subspace of itself</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Subspace</h><h>real <m>n</m>-space</h></idx>
      <p>The set <m>\R^n</m> is a subspace of itself: indeed, it contains zero, and is closed under addition and scalar multiplication.</p>
    </observation>

    <observation>
      <idx><h>Subspace</h><h>zero</h></idx>
      <p>The set <m>\{0\}</m> containing only the zero vector is a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>: it contains zero, and if you add zero to itself or multiply it by a scalar, you always get zero.</p>
    </observation>

    <example>
      <title>A line through the origin</title>
      <p>A line <m>L</m> through the origin is a subspace.
      </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
