    <assemblage>
      <title>Subsets versus Subspaces</title>
      <idx><h>Subspace</h><h>versus subset</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Subset</h><h>versus subspace</h></idx>
      <p>
        A sub<em>set</em> of <m>\R^n</m> is any collection of vectors whatsoever.  For instance, the unit circle
        <md>
<mrow>C = \bigl\{ (x,y)\text{ in }\R^2 \bigm| x^2 + y^2 = 1 \bigr\}</mrow>
</md>
        is a subset of <m>\R^2</m>, but it is not a subspace.  In fact, all of the non-examples above are still subsets of <m>\R^n</m>.  A subspace is a subset that happens to satisfy the three additional defining properties.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      In order to verify that a subset of <m>\R^n</m> is in fact a subspace, one has to check the three defining properties.  That is, unless the subset has already been verified to be a subspace: see <xref ref="subspace-is-col-or-nul"/> below.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="subspaces-eg-byhand">
      <title>Verifying that a subset is a subspace</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>V = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix}
a \\
b
\end{pmatrix} \text{ in } \R^2 \bigm| 2a = 3b\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
          Verify that <m>V</m> is a subspace.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First we point out that the condition <q><m>2a = 3b</m></q> defines whether or not a vector is in <m>V</m>: that is, to say <m>{a\choose b}</m> is in <m>V</m> <em>means</em> that <m>2a = 3b</m>.  In other words, a vector is in <m>V</m> if twice its first coordinate equals three times its second coordinate.  So for instance, <m>{3\choose 2}</m> and <m>{1/2\choose 1/3}</m> are in <m>V</m>, but <m>{2\choose 3}</m> is not because <m>2\cdot 2\neq 3\cdot 3</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let us check the first property.  The subset <m>V</m> does contain the zero vector <m>{0\choose 0}</m>, because <m>2\cdot 0 = 3\cdot 0</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Next we check the second property.  To show that <m>V</m> is closed under addition, we have to check that for <em>any</em> vectors <m>u = {a\choose b}</m> and <m>v = {c\choose d}</m> in <m>V</m>, the sum <m>u+v</m> is in <m>V</m>.  Since we cannot assume anything else about <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>, we must treat them as <em>unknowns</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We have
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
a \\
b
\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}
c \\
d
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
a+c \\
b+d
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          To say that <m>a+c\choose b+d</m> is contained in <m>V</m> means that <m>2(a+c) = 3(b+d)</m>, or <m>2a + 2c = 3b + 3d</m>.  The one thing we are allowed to assume about <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> is that <m>2a = 3b</m> and <m>2c = 3d</m>, so we see that <m>u+v</m> is indeed contained in <m>V</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Next we check the third property.  To show that <m>V</m> is closed under scalar multiplication, we have to check that for any vector <m>v = {a\choose b}</m> in <m>V</m> and any scalar <m>c</m> in <m>\R</m>, the product <m>cv</m> is in <m>V</m>.  Again, we must treat <m>v</m> and <m>c</m> as unknowns.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>c\begin{pmatrix}
a \\
b
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
ca \\
cb
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          To say that <m>ca\choose cb</m> is contained in <m>V</m> means that <m>2(ca) = 3(cb)</m>, i.e., that <m>c\cdot 2a = c\cdot 3b</m>.  The one thing we are allowed to assume about <m>v</m> is that <m>2a = 3b</m>, so <m>cv</m> is indeed contained in <m>V</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Since <m>V</m> satisfies all three defining properties, it is a subspace.
          In fact, it is the line through the origin with slope <m>2/3</m>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \draw[help lines] (-3, -3) grid (3, 3);
    \draw[seq4] (-3,-2) -- (3,2);
    \point at (0,0);
    \node[seq4] at (1.5,.7) {$V$};
  \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Showing that a subset is not a subspace</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>V = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix}
a \\
b
\end{pmatrix} \text{ in } \R^2\bigm| ab=0 \right\}</mrow>
</md>.
          Is <m>V</m> a subspace?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First we point out that the condition <q><m>ab=0</m></q> defines whether or not a vector is in <m>V</m>: that is, to say <m>{a\choose b}</m> is in <m>V</m> <em>means</em> that <m>ab=0</m>.  In other words, a vector is in <m>V</m> if the product of its coordinates is zero, i.e., if one (or both) of its coordinates are zero.  So for instance, <m>{1\choose 0}</m> and <m>{0\choose 2}</m> are in <m>V</m>, but <m>{1\choose 1}</m> is not because <m>1\cdot 1\neq 0</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let us check the first property.  The subset <m>V</m> does contain the zero vector <m>{0\choose 0}</m>, because <m>0\cdot 0=0</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Next we check the third property.  To show that <m>V</m> is closed under scalar multiplication, we have to check that for <em>any</em> vector <m>v = {a\choose b}</m> in <m>V</m> and <em>any</em> scalar <m>c</m> in <m>\R</m>, the product <m>cv</m> is in <m>V</m>.  Since we cannot assume anything else about <m>v</m> and <m>c</m>, we must treat them as <em>unknowns</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We have
          <md>
<mrow>c\begin{pmatrix}
a \\
b
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
ca \\
cb
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          To say that <m>ca\choose cb</m> is contained in <m>V</m> means that <m>(ca)(cb)=0</m>.  Rewriting, this means <m>c^2(ab)=0</m>.  The one thing we are allowed to assume about <m>v</m> is that <m>ab=0</m>, so we see that <m>cv</m> is indeed contained in <m>V</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Next we check the second property.  It turns out that <m>V</m> is not closed under addition; to verify this, we must show that there exists <em>some</em> vectors <m>u,v</m> in <m>V</m> such that <m>u+v</m> is not contained in <m>V</m>.  The easiest way to do so is to produce examples of such vectors.  We can take <m>u={1\choose 0}</m> and <m>v={0\choose 1}</m>; these are contained in <m>V</m> because the products of their coordinates are zero, but
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          is not contained in <m>V</m> because <m>1\cdot 1\neq 0</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Since <m>V</m> does not satisfy the second property (it is not closed under addition), we conclude that <m>V</m> is <em>not</em> a subspace.  Indeed, it is the union of the two coordinate axes, which is not a span.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3]
  \draw[grid lines, step=.25] (-1,-1) grid (1,1);
  \draw[seq4, &lt;->, thick] (-1,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw[seq4, &lt;->, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,1);
  \node[seq4] at (.25,.25) {$V$};
\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-common-types-of-subspaces">
    <title>Common Types of Subspaces</title>

    <theorem xml:id="subspaces-spans-are-subspaces">
      <title>Spans are Subspaces and Subspaces are Spans</title>
      <idx><h>Subspace</h><h>and spans</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Span</h><h>is a subspace</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Subspace</h><h>is a span</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p</m> are any vectors in <m>\R^n</m>, then <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m> is a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>.  Moreover, any subspace of <m>\R^n</m> can be written as a span of a set of <m>p</m> linearly independent vectors in <m>\R^n</m> for <m>p\leq n</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <proof>
        <p>To show that <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m> is a subspace, we have to verify the three defining properties.
        <ol>
          <li><p>The zero vector <m>0 = 0v_1 + 0v_2 + \cdots + 0v_p</m> is in the span.</p></li>
          <li><p>
            If <m>u = a_1v_1 + a_2v_2 + \cdots + a_pv_p</m> and
            <m>v = b_1v_1 + b_2v_2 + \cdots + b_pv_p</m> are in <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m>, then
            <md>
<mrow>u + v = (a_1+b_1)v_1 + (a_2+b_2)v_2 + \cdots + (a_p+b_p)v_p</mrow>
</md>
            is also in <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            If <m>v = a_1v_1 + a_2v_2 + \cdots + a_pv_p</m> is in <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m> and <m>c</m> is a scalar, then
            <md>
<mrow>cv = ca_1v_1 + ca_2v_2 + \cdots + ca_pv_p</mrow>
</md>
            is also in <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m>.
          </p></li>
        </ol>
        Since <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m> satisfies the three defining properties of a subspace, it is a subspace.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now let <m>V</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>.  If <m>V</m> is the zero subspace, then it is the span of the empty set, so we may assume <m>V</m> is nonzero. Choose a nonzero vector <m>v_1</m> in <m>V</m>.  If <m>V = \Span\{v_1\}</m>, then we are done.  Otherwise, there exists a vector <m>v_2</m> that is in <m>V</m> but not in <m>\Span\{v_1\}</m>.  Then <m>\Span\{v_1,v_2\}</m> is contained in <m>V</m>, and by the <xref ref="linindep-increasing-span">increasing span criterion</xref>, the set <m>\{v_1,v_2\}</m> is linearly independent.  If <m>V = \Span\{v_1,v_2\}</m> then we are done.  Otherwise, we continue in this fashion until we have written <m>V = \Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m> for some linearly independent set <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m>.  This process terminates after at most <m>n</m> steps by <xref ref="linindep-wide-matrix"/>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>If <m>V = \Span\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m>, we say that <m>V</m> is the subspace <term>spanned by</term> or <term>generated by</term> the vectors <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p</m>.  We call <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_p\}</m> a <term>spanning set</term> for <m>V</m>.</p>

    <p>Any matrix naturally gives rise to <em>two</em> subspaces.</p>

    <definition>
      <idx><h>Column space</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Null space</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <notation><usage><m>\Col(A)</m></usage><description>Column space</description></notation>
      <notation><usage><m>\Nul(A)</m></usage><description>Null space</description></notation>
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix.
        <ul>
          <li><p>
            The <term>column space</term> of <m>A</m> is the subspace of <m>\R^m</m> spanned by the columns of <m>A</m>.  It is written <m>\Col(A)</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            The <term>null space</term> of <m>A</m> is the subspace of <m>\R^n</m> consisting of all solutions of the homogeneous equation <m>Ax=0</m>:
            <md>
<mrow>\Nul(A) = \bigl\{ x \text{ in } \R^n\bigm| Ax=0 \bigr\}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
        </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      <idx><h>Column space</h><h>is a subspace</h></idx>
      The column space is defined to be a span, so it is a subspace by the above <xref ref="subspaces-spans-are-subspaces"/>.  We need to verify that the null space is really a subspace.  In <xref ref="sec-solution-sets" text="type-global"/> we already saw that the set of solutions of <m>Ax=0</m> is always a span, so the fact that the null spaces is a subspace should not come as a surprise.
    </p>

    <proof>
      <idx><h>Null space</h><h>is a subspace</h></idx>
      <p>
        We have to verify the three defining properties.  To say that a vector <m>v</m> is in <m>\Nul(A)</m> <em>means</em> that <m>Av = 0</m>.
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            The zero vector is in <m>\Nul(A)</m> because <m>A0=0</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Suppose that <m>u,v</m> are in <m>\Nul(A)</m>.  This means that
            <m>Au=0</m> and <m>Av=0</m>.  Hence
            <md>
<mrow>A(u+v) = Au + Av = 0 + 0 = 0</mrow>
</md>
            by the <xref ref="matrix-linearity">linearity of the matrix-vector product</xref>.  Therefore, <m>u+v</m> is in <m>\Nul(A)</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Suppose that <m>v</m> is in <m>\Nul(A)</m> and <m>c</m> is a scalar.  Then
            <md>
<mrow>A(cv) = cAv = c\cdot 0 = 0</mrow>
</md>
            by the <xref ref="matrix-linearity">linearity of the matrix-vector product</xref>, so <m>cv</m> is also in <m>\Nul(A)</m>.
          </p></li>
        </ol>
        Since <m>\Nul(A)</m> satisfies the three defining properties of a subspace, it is a subspace.
      </p>
    </proof>

    <example xml:id="subspaces-col-nul-1">
      <statement>
        <p>Describe the column space and the null space of<md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The column space is the span of the columns of <m>A</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>\Col(A) = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}
          = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
          This is a line in <m>\R^3</m>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[myxyz, x={(-.3cm,-.4cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}]
  \path[clip, resetxy] (-4,-2) rectangle (4,2);

  \draw[seq4] (-4,-4,-4) -- (0,0,0);
   \begin{scope}[transformxy]
    \fill[white, semitransparent] (-1.5,-1.5) rectangle (1.5,1.5);
    \draw[step=1cm, grid lines] (-1.5,-1.5) grid (1.5,1.5);
  \end{scope}

  \draw[seq4] (4,4,4) -- (0,0,0);
  \draw[vector] (0,0,0) -- (1,1,1);
  \draw[densely dotted] (1,1,0) -- (1,1,1);
  \node[resetxy,seq4,below right] at (1.5,1.5) {$\Col(A)$};
  \point at (0,0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
        <p>
          The null space is the solution set of the homogeneous system <m>Ax=0</m>.  To compute this, we need to row reduce <m>A</m>.  Its reduced row echelon form is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          This gives the equation <m>x+y=0</m>, or
          <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
x \amp {}={} \amp -y \amp {} \\
y \amp {}={} \amp y \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
          \quad\xrightarrow{\text{parametric vector form}}\quad
          \begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{pmatrix} = y\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence the null space is <m>\Span\bigl\{{-1\choose 1}\bigr\}</m>, which is a line in <m>\R^2</m>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \draw[grid lines] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
  \draw[seq4] (3,-3) -- (-3,3);
  \draw[vector] (0,0) -- (-1,1);
  \point at (0,0);
  \node[seq4,fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (-.7,2) {$\Nul(A)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
        <p>
          Notice that the column space is a subspace of <m>\R^3</m>, whereas the null space is a subspace of <m>\R^2</m>.  This is because <m>A</m> has three rows and two columns.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      The column space and the null space of a matrix are both subspaces, so they are both spans.  The column space of a matrix <m>A</m> is defined to be the span of the columns of <m>A</m>.  The null space is defined to be the solution set of <m>Ax=0</m>, so this is a good example of a kind of subspace that we can define <em>without any spanning set in mind.</em>  In other words, it is easier to show that the null space is a subspace than to show it is a span<mdash/>see the proof above.  In order to do computations, however, it is usually necessary to find a spanning set.
    </p>

    <observation>
      <title>Null Spaces are Solution Sets</title>
    <p>
      <idx><h>Null space</h><h>is a solution set</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Solution set</h><h>of a homogeneous system is a null space</h></idx>
      The null space of a matrix is the solution set of a homogeneous system of equations.  For example, the null space of the matrix
      <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 7 \amp 2 \\
-2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
4 \amp -2 \amp -3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
      is the solution set of <m>Ax=0</m>, i.e., the solution set of the system of equations
      <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{alignedat}{4}
x \amp {}+{} \amp 7y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp {} \\
-2x \amp {}+{} \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 3z \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp {} \\
4x \amp {}-{} \amp 2y \amp {}-{} \amp 3z \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right.</mrow>
</md>
      Conversely, the solution set of any homogeneous system of equations is precisely the null space of the corresponding coefficient matrix.
    </p>
    </observation>

    <p>
      To find a spanning set for the null space, one has to solve a system of homogeneous equations.
    </p>

    <algorithm xml:id="subspaces-span-nullspace">
      <title>Compute a spanning set for a null space</title>
      <idx><h>Null space</h><h>computing</h></idx>
      <p>
        To find a spanning set for <m>\Nul(A)</m>, compute the parametric vector form of the solutions to the homogeneous equation <m>Ax=0</m>.  The vectors attached to the free variables form a spanning set for <m>\Nul(A)</m>.
      </p>
    </algorithm>

    <example>
      <title>Two free variables</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Find a spanning set for the null space of the matrix
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 3 \amp -8 \amp -5 \\
-1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \amp -8
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We compute the parametric vector form of the solutions of <m>Ax=0</m>.  The reduced row echelon form of <m>A</m> is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp -3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The free variables are <m>x_3</m> and <m>x_4</m>; the parametric form of the solution set is
          <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
x_1 \amp {}={} \amp x_3 - 2x_4 \amp {} \\
x_2 \amp {}={} \amp 2x_3 + 3x_4 \amp {} \\
x_3 \amp {}={} \amp x_3 \amp {} \\
x_4 \amp {}={} \amp x_4 \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
            \quad\xrightarrow[\text{vector form}]{\text{parametric}}\quad
            \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3 \\
x_4
\end{pmatrix} = x_3\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
2 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + x_4\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
3 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore,
          <md>
<mrow>\Nul(A) = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
2 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
3 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>No free variables</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Find a spanning set for the null space of the matrix
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 3 \\
2 \amp 4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We compute the parametric vector form of the solutions of <m>Ax=0</m>.  The reduced row echelon form of <m>A</m> is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          There are no free variables; hence the only solution of <m>Ax=0</m> is the trivial solution.  In other words,
          <md>
<mrow>\Nul(A) = \{0\} = \Span\{0\}</mrow>
</md>.
          It is natural to define <m>\Span\{\} = \{0\}</m>, so that we can take our spanning set to be empty.  This is consistent with the <xref ref="dimension-defn-dimension">definition of dimension</xref>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Writing a subspace as a column space or a null space</title>
      <idx><h>Subspace</h><h>writing as column or null space</h></idx>

      <p>
        A subspace can be given to you in many different forms.  In practice, computations involving subspaces are much easier if your subspace is the column space or null space of a matrix.  The simplest example of such a computation is finding a spanning set: a column space is by definition the span of the columns of a matrix, and we showed above how to compute a spanning set for a null space using parametric vector form.  For this reason, it is useful to rewrite a subspace as a column space or a null space before trying to answer questions about it.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="subspace-is-col-or-nul">
        <p>
          When asking questions about a subspace, it is usually best to rewrite the subspace as a column space or a null space.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        This also applies to the question <q>is my subset a subspace?</q>  If your subset is a column space or null space of a matrix, then the answer is yes.
      </p>

      <observation>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>V = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix}
a \\
b
\end{pmatrix} \text{ in } \R^2 \bigm| 2a = 3b\right\}</mrow>
</md>
          be the subset of a previous <xref ref="subspaces-eg-byhand"/>.  The subset <m>V</m> is exactly the solution set of the homogeneous equation <m>2x - 3y = 0</m>.
          Therefore,
          <md>
<mrow>V = \Nul\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          In particular, it is a subspace.  The reduced row echelon form of <m>\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -3
\end{pmatrix}</m> is <m>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -3/2
\end{pmatrix}</m>, so the parametric form of <m>V</m> is <m>x = 3/2y</m>, so the parametric vector form is <m>{x\choose y} = y{3/2\choose 1},</m> and hence <m>\bigl\{{3/2\choose 1}\bigr\}</m> spans <m>V</m>.
        </p>
      </observation>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>V</m> be the plane in <m>\R^3</m> defined by
            <md>
<mrow>V = \left\{\begin{pmatrix}
2x+y \\
x-y \\
3x-2y
\end{pmatrix}\bigm| x,y \text{ are in } \R \right\}</mrow>
</md>.
            Write <m>V</m> as the column space of a matrix.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Since
            <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
2x+y \\
x-y \\
3x-2y
\end{pmatrix} = x\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
1 \\
3
\end{pmatrix} + y\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-1 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
            we notice that <m>V</m> is exactly the span of the vectors
            <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
1 \\
3
\end{pmatrix} \sptxt{and} \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-1 \\
-2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
            Hence
            <md>
<mrow>V = \Col\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1 \\
3 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

    </paragraphs>

  </subsection>
<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>The set of solutions to a consistent matrix equation is
      always a subspace.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  The set of solutions to a consistent matrix equation <m>Ax = b</m> is
      only a subspace when <m>b=0</m>.  If <m>b</m> is not the zero vector, then the
      solution set to <m>Ax = b</m> will not contain the zero vector.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>The set of <m>{\bf R}^n</m> that consists only of the zero
      vector is a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^n</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  The set <m>\{0\}</m> contains the zero vector, and it is closed under
      addition and scalar multiplication because any linear combination of the
      <m>0</m> vector is itself the zero vector.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is a <m>9 \times 15</m> matrix, then the null space of
      <m>A</m> is a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^9</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False: <m>\Nul(A)</m> is a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^{15}</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>Every subspace of <m>{\bf R}^n</m> is the span of some
        vectors in <m>{\bf R}^n</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  It is a fundamental fact that every subspace of
          <m>{\bf R}^n</m> is the span of some vectors in <m>{\bf R}^n</m>, and,
          conversely, the span of any number of vectors in <m>{\bf R}^n</m> is a
          subspace of <m>{\bf R}^n</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider the set <m>S = \{(x, y) \textup{ in } {\bf R}^2 \ | \ xy \geq 0\}</m>.  Is
      <m>S</m> a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^2</m>?  If not, which properties does it fail?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>(1) <m>S</m> contains the zero vector, since <m>0\cdot 0 = 0 \geq 0</m>.</p>
      <p>(2) <m>S</m> is not closed under addition.  For example, take <m>u = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> and
        <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Both vectors are in <m>S</m> since <m>1\cdot 2 > 0</m> and
        <m>(-2)(-1) = 2 >0</m>.  However, their sum is <m>u+v = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>, which is not in
        <m>S</m> since <m>(-1)(1)\lt 0</m>.</p>
      <p>
        (3) <m>S</m> is closed under scalar multiplication.  If <m>u = \begin{pmatrix}x \\ y\end{pmatrix}</m> is in <m>S</m>
          and <m>c</m> is any scalar, then for <m>cu = \begin{pmatrix}cx \\ cy\end{pmatrix}</m> we have
        <md><mrow>(cx)(cy) = c^2 (xy) \geq 0 \quad \textup{ since } c^2 \geq 0 \textup{ and } xy \geq 0</mrow></md>.
      </p>
      <p>Most students find it easier if we first draw a picture of <m>S</m> to gain
          intuition, rather than plugging in random vectors to test properties.  In
          order for a vector to be in <m>S</m>, either both entries are positive, or both
          entries are negative, or at least one entry is zero.  Putting this together,
          we see that <m>S</m> is the union of the first and third quadrants of the
          <m>xy</m>-plane (including the <m>x</m>-axis and <m>y</m>-axis).</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider the <m>x</m>-axis in <m>{\bf R}^3</m>.  Is it a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^3</m>?  If not,
      which properties does it fail?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Yes: the <m>x</m>-axis in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> is the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m>, and spans are
        automatically subspaces of the appropriate <m>{\bf R}^n</m>how, so the <m>x</m>-axis is a
        subspace of <m>{\bf R}^3</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider the set
      <m>S = \{(x, y, z, w) \textup{ in } {\bf R}^4 \ | \ x + y - z + w = 0\}</m>.  Is <m>S</m>
      a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^4</m>?  If not, which properties does it fail?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Yes: <m>S</m> is the solution set to the augmented system <m>\left(\begin{array}{rrrr|r}1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</m>,
        which is precisely <m>\Nul\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Null spaces are always subspaces of
        the appropriate <m>{\bf R}^{n}</m>, and in this case <m>S</m> is the null space of a
        matrix with <m>4</m> columns, so <m>S</m> is a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^{4}</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>W</m> be the green graph below.  Determine which properties
      of a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^2</m> are satisfied by <m>W</m>.</p>
      <image source="images/2_6/2_6-lineorigin">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>(1) <m>W</m> contains the zero vector, since the line passes through the origin.</p>
      <p>(2) <m>W</m> is closed under addition.  If <m>v</m> and <m>w</m> are vectors along the line
        <m>W</m>, their sum is just another vector along the line.</p>
      <p>(3) <m>W</m> is closed under scalar multiplication.  If you scale any vector along
        <m>W</m>, the result is still a vector along <m>W</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let
      <m>V = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix}a \\ b \\ c\end{pmatrix} \ \textup{ in } {\bf R}^3 \ : \ a-b = c\right\}</m>.  Is
      <m>V</m> a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^3</m>?  Justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Yes: <m>V</m> is the set of solutions to the homogeneous system of equations
        <m>a-b-c = 0</m>.  In other words, <m>V = \Nul\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -1 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  The null space
        of any matrix with <m>3</m> columns is a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^3</m>.</p>
      <p>Alternatively, we could verify the subspace properties individually.
        For example, <m>V</m> contains <m>\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> since <m>0 - 0 = 0</m>, and we
        could similarly verify that <m>V</m> is closed under addition and
        multiplication.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Which of the following are subspaces of <m>{\bf R}^4</m>?  Answer yes or nox
      in each case.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>\Col\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 3 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \\ 4 \amp 3 \amp 2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No.  This is a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^3</m>, not <m>{\bf R}^4</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>\Nul\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4\end{pmatrix}</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Yes.  The null space of any <m>3 \times 4</m> matrix is a
          subspace of <m>{\bf R}^4</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The set of solutions to the system of equations in the
            variables <m>x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4</m> given by
          <md><mrow>x_1 - x_2 + x_3 + x_4 = 1</mrow></md>
          <md><mrow>x_1 + x_4 = 2</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No.  This solution set does not contain the origin,
          since it corresponds to a non-homogeneous linear system of
          equations.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>If <m>V</m> and <m>W</m> are sets in <m>{\bf R}^n</m>, then their \textit{union},
      denoted <m>V \cup W</m>, is the set of all vectors that are in at least one of <m>V</m>
      and <m>W</m>.  In other words, a vector <m>x</m> is in <m>V \cup W</m> if <m>x</m> is in <m>V</m> or <m>x</m>
      is in <m>W</m> (or <m>x</m> is in both <m>V</m> and <m>W</m>).</p>
      <p>Suppose <m>U</m> and <m>W</m> are subspaces of <m>{\bf R}^n</m>.  When will <m>U \cup W</m> also a
      subspace of <m>{\bf R}^n</m>?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The union will be a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^n</m> if and only if one of one of <m>U</m>
        or <m>V</m> is contained in the other (or both are equal).  In this case, the union
        <m>U \cup V</m> will just be one of <m>U</m> or <m>V</m>, which we already know to be
        subspaces.</p>
      <p>
        On the other hand, suppose neither <m>U</m> nor <m>V</m> is contained in the other.  Then there is a vector <m>u</m> that is in <m>U</m> but not in <m>V</m>, and there is a vector <m>v</m> that is in <m>V</m> but not in <m>U</m>.  We show that:
        <md><mrow>u+v \textup{ is not in } U \cup V</mrow></md>.
        After all, <m>u+v</m> cannot be in the
        subspace <m>U</m>, because if it were then <m>(u+v)-u = v</m> would need to be in <m>U</m>
        since <m>U</m> is closed under addition.  Similarly, <m>u+v</m> cannot be in <m>V</m> because
        then <m>(u+v)-v = u</m> would need to be in <m>V</m> since <m>V</m> is closed under addition.
      </p>
      <p>In summary: <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are in <m>U \cup V</m>, but <m>u+v</m> is not in <m>U \cup V</m>, so
      <m>U \cup V</m> is not closed under addition and is therefore not a subspace of
      <m>{\bf R}^n</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>W</m> be the green graph below.  Determine which properties
      of a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^2</m> are satisfied by <m>W</m>.</p>
      <image source="images/2_6/2_6-which.pdf">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>W</m> doesn't satisfy any of the properties of a subspace:</p>
      <p>(1) <m>W</m> does not pass through the origin.</p>
      <p>(2) <m>W</m> is not closed under addition since for example <m>(-2,0)</m> and
        <m>(0, 2)</m> are in <m>W</m> but their sum <m>(-2,2)</m> is not in <m>W</m>.</p>
      <p>(3) <m>W</m> is not closed under multiplication since, for example,
        <m>(-2, 0)</m> is in <m>W</m> but <m>-(-2,0)=(2, 0)</m> is not in <m>W</m>.</p>
      <p>Although it fails all subspace properties, the truth is that <m>W</m> is
        <em>almost</em> a subspace because it is a line in <m>{\bf R}^2</m>, but
        the fact that it does not pass through the origin ruins all three
        subspace properties.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>W</m> be the red graph below.  It is the paraboloid
      <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>. Determine which properties of a subspace of
      <m>{\bf R}^3</m> are satisfied by <m>W</m>.</p>
      <image source="images/2_6/paraboloid">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>(1) <m>W</m> contains the zero vector since it passes through the
        origin.</p>
      <p>(2) <m>W</m> is not closed under scalar multiplication because if <m>u</m> is
        any nonzero vector in <m>W</m> then it is above the <m>z</m>-axis, but <m>-u</m> is
        below the <m>z</m>-axis and thus not in <m>W</m>.</p>
      <p>(3) <m>W</m> is not closed under addition because, for example, the point
        <m>(0, 1, 1)</m> is in <m>W</m> but <m>(0, 2, 2)</m> is not in <m>W</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>W</m> be the light blue graph below.  Determine which
      properties of a subspace of <m>{\bf R}^3</m> are satisfied by <m>W</m>.</p>
      <image source="images/2_6/planeno0">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>W</m> is a plane in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> that does <em>not</em> contain the
        origin.  It will fail all three properties of a subspace in the same
        way that a line not through the origin in <m>{\bf R}^2</m> will fail all
        three subspace properties.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Recall from section 2.1 that a QR code is a
      <m>29 \times 29</m> grid of boxes, where each box is colored black or
      white.  We can think of each box as labeled by <m>1</m> (if white) or <m>0</m>
      (if black).  Reading from top to bottom and left to right, we can
      regard a QR code as a vector <m>x</m> in
      <m>{\bf R}^{29 \times 29} = {\bf R}^{841}</m>, where each entry of <m>x</m> is
      <m>0</m> or <m>1</m>.</p>
      <p>Let <m>S</m> be the set of all QR codes, considered as a subset of
      <m>{\bf R}^{841}</m>.  Determine which properties of a subspace <m>S</m>
      satisfies.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Does <m>S</m> contain the <m>0</m> vector?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Yes.  The vector <m>v = (0, 0, \ldots, 0)</m> is the QR
            code where every box is black.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Is <m>S</m>
          closed under addition?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No.  The vectors <m>x = (1, 0, 0, \ldots 0)</m> and
            <m>y=(1, 1, 0, \ldots, 0)</m> are in <m>S</m>.  However, if we add <m>x</m> and
            <m>y</m> we obtain <m>x+y = (2, 1, 0, \ldots, 0)</m>, which is not in <m>S</m>
            because it has an entry (its first entry) which is neither <m>0</m> nor
            <m>1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Is <m>S</m> closed under scalar
          multiplication?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No.  The vector <m>x = (1, 0, 0, \ldots, 0)</m> is in <m>S</m>
            but <m>2x = (2, 0, 0, \ldots, 0)</m> is not in <m>S</m> because it has an
            entry (its first entry) which is neither <m>0</m> nor <m>1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>V</m> be the set of all points <m>(x, y)</m> in <m>{\bf R}^2</m>
      satisfying <m>x - y > 1</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Does <m>V</m> contain the zero vector?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No, since <m>0-0</m> is not greater than <m>1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Is <m>V</m> closed under addition?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Yes: take any vectors <m>u = \begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ y_1\end{pmatrix}</m> and
              <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}x_2 \\ y_2\end{pmatrix}</m> in <m>V</m>, so <m>x_1 - y_1 \geq 1</m>.  For the vector
              <m>u+v = \begin{pmatrix}x_1+x_2 \\ y_1+y_2\end{pmatrix}</m>, we subtract the second coordinate
              from the first and regroup terms:
          <md>
            <mrow>(x_1+x_2)-(y_1+y_2)
                \amp   = x_1+x_2 - y_1 - y_2</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp   = (x_1 - y_1) + (x_2 - y_2)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp  > 1 + 1 > 1</mrow>
          </md>,
          therefore <m>u+v</m> is in <m>V</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Is <m>V</m> closed under scalar multiplication?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No: for example, <m>u = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> is in <m>V</m>, but
            <m>-u = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> is not in <m>V</m> since <m>-1 - 0 = -1</m> which is not
            greater than <m>1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <solution>
      <p>An alternative approach is to draw <m>V</m>, which is relatively
        straightforward since <m>V</m> consists of all points satisfying
        <m>y \lt x - 1</m>, which is all points that are strictly below the line
        <m>y = x-1</m>.  Once you graph <m>V</m>, you can tell right away that it does
        not contain the zero vector and is not closed under scalar
        multiplication, and you can also deduce that it is closed under
        addition.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>V</m> be the set of all points <m>(x, y, z)</m> in <m>{\bf R}^3</m>
        satisfying <m>xyz \geq 0</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Does <m>V</m> contain the zero vector?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Yes: the origin satisfies
              <m>0\cdot 0 \cdot 0 = 0 \geq 0</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Is <m>V</m> closed under addition?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          No: for example, let
          <md><mrow>u = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -1 \\ -1\end{pmatrix} \qquad v = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
          Then <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>
                are both in <m>V</m> but their sum is <m>u+v = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ -1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> which is
                not in <m>V</m> since <m>(2)(-1)(1) = -2 \lt 0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Is <m>V</m> closed under scalar multiplication?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>(Challenge problem) Abstract spaces go beyond just
        <m>{\bf R}^n</m>.  For example, the set of all polynomials with real
        coefficients forms an abstract vector space.</p>
      <p>
        Consider the set <m>W</m> consisting of all polynomials that have degree
          <m>2</m> or less.  In other words, <m>W</m> is the set of all functions of the
          form
        <md><mrow>f(x) = a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2</mrow></md>
        where <m>a_0</m>, <m>a_1</m>, and <m>a_2</m> are real numbers.
          Show that <m>W</m> satisfies the properties of a subspace:
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p><m>W</m> contains the zero polynomial.  In other words, <m>W</m>
            contains the polynomial given by <m>p(x) = 0</m> for all <m>x</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If polynomials <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are in <m>W</m>, then so is the
              polynomial <m>f+g</m> given by
          <md><mrow>(f+g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>f</m> is in <m>W</m> and <m>c</m> is a scalar, then the
            polynomial <m>cf</m> given by <m>(cf)(x) = cf(x)</m> is also in <m>W</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
</section>
